                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_business:      `<strong>That is the right reframe.</strong> You turned a fuzzy learning brief into a measurable business outcome — cycle-time and decision speed during the transformation. Now every later design choice has a target to aim at, and Pennine can see in numbers whether it worked.`,
                    coach_r1_learning:      `<strong>“Stronger leadership capability” is a learning objective, not a business outcome.</strong> It is a step in the right direction, but Pennine will ask what capability is <em>for</em>. State the metric you intend to move — cycle-time, decision speed — or you cannot design backward from it.`,
                    coach_r1_satisfaction:  `<strong>Your end-of-programme scores will be 4.8 out of 5 — participants will love it.</strong> But Pennine told you they are measuring cycle-time and decision speed, not happiness. Eighteen months from now their sponsor asks “what changed in the business?” and you have testimonials, not a metric.`,
                    coach_r1_product:       `<strong>You have pitched your standard curriculum.</strong> Pennine's whole reason for buying is a regulator-mandated operating-model change. A programme that ignores their actual transformation is a course, not a partnership — and the consultancy across the table is selling exactly the alignment you skipped.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> A measurable business outcome AND a Level 4 results target — that is Jonsson's standard. You have defined both the number you will move and the proof you will show, and that is the spine the whole programme now hangs from.`,
                    coach_r1_level2:        `<strong>Level 2 stops at the classroom.</strong> Knowing more is not the same as the business changing. Pennine is buying Levels 3 and 4 — behaviour at work and a moved business result. Aim the proof there.`,
                    coach_r1_reaction:      `<strong>Level 1 is the easy sale and the wrong one.</strong> Reaction scores are why most programmes are sold and why most prove nothing. Pennine has explicitly ruled satisfaction out. Commit to behaviour and results.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigour:     `<strong>Low commitment to the measured outcome is how the happy-sheet creeps back in.</strong> Half-hearted about impact and you will quietly default to the programme that is easy and well-liked — and lose to the bidder who took impact seriously.`,
                    coach_r2_backward:      `<strong>Designing backward is exactly right.</strong> Mapping every module to a Pennine metric means there is no decorative content and no orphaned outcome — each part of the programme has a business job to do. This is what separates a partnership from a course.`,
                    coach_r2_modular:       `<strong>Good modules, weak spine.</strong> Assembling best-practice content and sequencing it is competent, but it starts from your catalogue, not Pennine's outcome. Some of it will move their number and some won't, and you won't know which. Design backward.`,
                    coach_r2_flagship:      `<strong>A signature experience is a satisfaction play.</strong> Marquee speakers and a beautiful residential maximise reaction scores and minimise transfer. You are optimising the metric Pennine told you they are not buying.`,
                    coach_r2_catalogue:     `<strong>An off-the-shelf curriculum ignores their transformation.</strong> Pennine is buying alignment to a specific regulator-mandated change; a standard programme is the course the consultancy is betting you will pitch. Build around their challenge.`,
                    coach_r2_aligned:       `<strong>Backward design plus real measurement — this is the win condition.</strong> A baseline, a comparison and an 18-month follow-through means that when the board asks “what changed?” you have an attributable answer, not a story. That is how exec ed proves impact.`,
                    coach_r2_endsurvey:     `<strong>An end survey and a testimonial reel measure feelings, not impact.</strong> It evidences Level 1 and nothing more. When Pennine's procurement lead asks how you will know at month 18, a satisfaction survey is not an answer.`,
                    coach_r2_nomeasure:     `<strong>No measurement is a programme you can never prove worked.</strong> Anecdotes will not survive the board's “show me the number” — and without evidence there is no renewal. Build the baseline and follow-through in now; it is far harder to bolt on later.`,
                    coach_r2_lowalign:      `<strong>Loosely-coupled modules dilute impact.</strong> If components aren't each wired to a Pennine metric, effort leaks into content that is interesting but inert. Tighten the link from every module to the number it is meant to move.`,
                    coach_r3_blended:       `<strong>Blended-plus-coaching gets learning into the work.</strong> A digital core with applied projects and one-to-one coaching drives transfer at a sensible cost — strong impact without the day-rate burden of residential. A deliberate, well-judged choice.`,
                    coach_r3_hybrid:        `<strong>A hybrid with an embedded capstone is your highest-impact route.</strong> A blended core keeps the cost sane while a live-project capstone forces transfer into Pennine's real transformation. Powerful — provided you have actually staffed the embedded load.`,
                    coach_r3_embedded:      `<strong>Embedded action-learning is the most differentiated, highest-impact model.</strong> Learning happens on live Pennine projects, which is exactly the transfer they want — but it consumes faculty time you do not have spare. The model is only as good as the capacity plan behind it.`,
                    coach_r3_residential:   `<strong>Residential gives you the best day-rate margin and the nicest brochure.</strong> But Pennine's managers go back to work on Monday and nothing in their job has changed — the learning evaporates. You have optimised your margin and missed the impact you will be judged on.`,
                    coach_r3_capacityplan:  `<strong>A high-impact model with a real staffing plan — that is deliberate.</strong> Hiring programme managers, contracting external practitioner-faculty and trimming low-margin open dates is how a lean team delivers a high-touch programme without the wheels coming off mid-cohort.`,
                    coach_r3_stretch:       `<strong>“We'll manage on goodwill” is how delivery slips.</strong> Even a lighter model stretches a team already running 45 programmes. Name the backfill and the trade-off, or quality drifts and your other clients feel it.`,
                    coach_r3_stretch_hightouch: `<strong>A high-touch model on goodwill alone will break.</strong> Embedded and hybrid models consume faculty time your 34-person team does not have. “We'll manage” means the programme slips and the impact dies. Who actually delivers this, and which clients get less of you?`,
                    coach_r3_noplan:        `<strong>Deciding staffing later is deciding it badly.</strong> Capacity is not a detail to bolt on after the design — it is part of whether the design is deliverable at all. Commit to a plan now.`,
                    coach_r3_noplan_hightouch: `<strong>A high-touch model with no capacity plan is the classic blindness.</strong> Your 34 staff are already running 45 programmes; choosing embedded or hybrid “and deciding staffing later” is how the most differentiated option becomes the one that collapses. Plan the faculty now.`,
                    coach_r3_internalonly:  `<strong>Internal-only faculty limits transfer.</strong> External practitioners and coaches bring the live-context credibility and the one-to-one application that move behaviour at work. Lean too far on internal teaching staff and the programme stays in the classroom.`,
                    coach_r4_attributable:  `<strong>Measurable, attributable KPIs — exactly what makes this defensible.</strong> Cycle-time and decision speed, baselined, with attribution agreed up front, are within your legitimate influence and provable at month 18. That is a scorecard you can sign and a renewal you can win.`,
                    coach_r4_business:      `<strong>Hard KPIs, but no baseline or attribution is half a commitment.</strong> Procurement will ask: against what baseline, and how much of the result is the programme's? Without that agreed up front you have signed up to a number you cannot fairly be measured against — or defend.`,
                    coach_r4_vague:         `<strong>You have committed to “enhanced leadership capability.”</strong> Pennine's procurement lead asks: how will we know, in numbers, at month 18? If you can't answer, you have sold a programme you can never prove worked — and you won't renew.`,
                    coach_r4_satisfaction:  `<strong>Satisfaction targets are the metric Pennine explicitly ruled out.</strong> A 4.8 and a great NPS prove your managers enjoyed it. They will not survive the board's “what changed in the business?” — and committing to them now tells procurement you weren't listening.`,
                    coach_r4_value:         `<strong>Defending on impact and value is the right posture.</strong> Showing the margin math, the measurement design and why each KPI is within your influence answers procurement on their own terms — and it beats a consultancy selling a cheaper, softer promise.`,
                    coach_r4_prestige:      `<strong>Brand and rankings open the door; they don't win this room.</strong> Pennine's procurement lead has heard every school's ranking pitch. Lean on prestige and you cede the impact argument to the consultancy. Defend the measurable result instead.`,
                    coach_r4_discount:      `<strong>You priced at £1.25M to beat the consultancy — below your cost-plus-margin line.</strong> You have bought the logo and a loss. Exec ed lives on its 34% contribution margin to fund faculty and the open portfolio. What did you cut to hit that price — and was it the impact?`,
                    coach_r4_walk:          `<strong>Refusing to move at all reads as arrogance.</strong> Pennine has three other credible bidders; a flat “our price or nothing” hands them an easy reason to choose someone more collaborative. Defend the value — don't stonewall.`,
                    coach_r4_loss:          `<strong>At this price the margin is gone.</strong> A contribution margin in single figures cannot fund the faculty and open portfolio that the division depends on. Winning Pennine by losing money on it is not a win.`,

